            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Holds a set of numbers that are to be learned rather than set by hand: the strength of a correction, the offsets of a set of sensors, the coefficients of a curve. It sends them as a tensor that remembers where it came from, so whatever is worked out from them by torch nodes - a corrected pose, a fitted curve, and finally a loss that measures how wrong it is - can be traced back to them. t.optimize does that tracing and nudges the numbers to make the loss smaller.\n\nThe numbers can be kept in a .pt file: give a path and they are read back whenever the patch is opened, so what was learned survives a restart. Save writes them; t.optimize can also save them as it goes.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.param <shape ...> <device> <path>\nDefault: one number, 0, on the cpu. t.param 3 is three numbers; t.param 18 5 a table\nof 18 rows of 5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nAnything sends the numbers. A list or tensor of numbers sets them first (and gives them its shape, if that differs), then sends them. Wire t.optimize's 'again' outlet here.\n\nlearn (checkbox, also an inlet):\nOn (the default), t.optimize may change these numbers. Off, they are held still while others learn.\n\nsave (button):\nWrites the numbers to the path, or asks where if there is none.\n\nreset (button):\nPuts every number back to the initial value.\n\ninitial value:\nAn option, under the node. What reset puts back; 0 by default.\n\nlow, high:\nOptions. If high is above low, the numbers are kept between them: they ease toward either end rather than being cut off there, so they can approach a limit but never pass it. Both 0 (the default): no limits.\n\ndevice:\nAn option. Where the numbers live: cpu, or mps for the GPU. Everything worked out from them follows.\n\npath:\nAn option. The .pt file the numbers are kept in. If it exists when the patch opens, or when a path is entered, the numbers are read from it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nvalues:\nThe numbers, as a tensor. While learning, it carries the trail back to this node;\nwhile not, it is plain values and costs nothing extra downstream.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThree numbers learn to match 1 2 3: t.mse_loss measures how far they are from the target, and t.optimize moves them closer every frame, asking for a new loss through 'again'. Click the message to throw them to 10 -4 0.5 and watch them find their way back.\n\nRELATED:\nt.optimize does the learning. t.mse_loss and t.l1_loss measure how wrong a result is. t.save and t.load keep any tensor in a file.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1100,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
